WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.838
It's the world's biggest luxury company,
valued at more than $400 billion

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:08.174
with some of the industry's
biggest names under its umbrella.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:12.762
Dior, Louis Vuitton, Moët, Bulgari, Loewe, Tiffany,

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.557
It's definitely the 
dominant luxury player.

00:00:15.557 --> 00:00:19.060
Absolutely the leader of 
the luxury goods industry.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:24.149
Fashion, luxury hotels, media 
outlets all under one roof.

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:27.360
Led by one of the world's richest people.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.946
LVMH touched the $500 billion mark,

00:00:29.946 --> 00:00:32.198
making it the first European
company to do that.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.576
So how did a young 
man's vision in the 80s

00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:36.870
lead him to founding Europe's
most valuable company

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.247
and which of his five
children will succeed him?

00:00:39.247 --> 00:00:41.416
The story of LVMH is 
very interesting because

00:00:41.416 --> 00:00:44.294
it's the story of this company,
but also the story of this man, 

00:00:44.294 --> 00:00:45.086
Bernard Arnault.

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:55.597
LVMH Moet Hennessy Louis Vuitton

00:00:55.597 --> 00:00:56.848
has soared to become one of world's

00:00:56.848 --> 00:00:59.392
most valuable publicly 
traded companies,

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:02.187
dwarfing its rivals Hermes as well 
as Kering - the company that owns

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:05.231
Gucci, Yves Saint Laurent 
and Balenciaga.

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:08.860
The man behind this staggering 
increase in value is Bernard Arnault.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.529
He gained the moniker
"the wolf in a cashmere coat"

00:01:11.529 --> 00:01:12.697
thanks to his high stakes

00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:14.157
take no prisoners approach

00:01:14.157 --> 00:01:15.575
that helped him win control

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:17.160
of more than 70 brands.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.330
But beneath the tough
exterior was a leader with a vision.

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.208
Bernard Arnault set out to 
build a luxury supergroup

00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:26.419
that would maintain many
brands under one company.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:30.131
The idea seemed foolish to
many in the industry in the 1980s.

00:01:30.131 --> 00:01:33.384
How could one company
hold many competing brands?

00:01:33.384 --> 00:01:34.886
And where would he find them all?

00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:39.432
The first stepping stone of his business
history, I would say, was Christian Dior.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.518
Christian Dior was a 
gem that he spotted

00:01:41.518 --> 00:01:43.812
that was part of a textile company
that was going bankrupt.

00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:46.439
Bernard Arnault knew that 
Dior had the potential

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.567
to become a brand that was 
going to become globalized.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:50.819
If you look at all the luxury groups

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:53.905
and specifically LVMH, there are ways

00:01:53.905 --> 00:01:55.115
of strategically transforming

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:56.366
in the industry

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:59.869
and for LVMH, it was always about M&amp;A

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:01.663
sometimes aggressive M&amp;A.

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:05.333
There has been a huge
wave of brand acquisitions.

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:09.504
The idea being to build a
big group as fast as possible.

00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:12.799
But the biggest and boldest of LVMH’s
acquisitions was American jeweler

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:15.176
Tiffany's in November 2019.

00:02:15.176 --> 00:02:20.014
At almost $16 billion it remains the luxury industry's biggest takeover.

00:02:20.014 --> 00:02:21.808
But it wasn't without 
its share of drama.

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:23.893
LVMH has said here and there

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:25.353
that they were not particularly

00:02:25.353 --> 00:02:27.188
interested in that asset.

00:02:27.188 --> 00:02:29.899
So clearly this came out as a surprise.

00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:32.986
So in 2020 Covid hits,
and basically people

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:34.988
are buying fewer luxury goods,

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:36.447
they cannot go to the stores,

00:02:36.447 --> 00:02:37.490
they are stuck at home.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.492
At some point that there was a lot

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.494
of tense and protracted negotiations.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.163
They threatened to go to court

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:44.789
and in the end, they settled.

00:02:44.789 --> 00:02:47.375
When it completed in 2021,

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:48.626
what we ended up with 

00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:50.753
was a $16 billion deal

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:54.507
something which was four times
anything we'd really seen in the industry

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.050
outside of LVMH.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.678
But if we think about where
both companies were

00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:10.857
For LVMH, with Tiffany

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:14.319
it really opened up the Asian market
more in its luxury division.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.864
LVMH’s business strategy, together
with a budding middle class

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:21.951
and Asia's fast accumulation of wealth
through the 1990s and 2000s,

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:23.578
provided the prime conditions

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:26.581
for the company's rapid
growth and past successes.

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:28.041
What has happened in China

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:29.375
in the last 20 or 30 years

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.211
is you've had this increasingly large,

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:32.795
wealthy, middle or upper class

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:35.798
who have been engaged
in conspicuous consumption.

00:03:35.798 --> 00:03:37.383
That's been good for luxury goods.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:41.846
What's so strong about LVMH is that
you have a lot of economies of scale

00:03:41.846 --> 00:03:44.641
because they have so many 
brands, they're able to negotiate

00:03:44.641 --> 00:03:48.186
better leases with landlords
when it comes to their store locations.

00:03:48.186 --> 00:03:51.022
And they not only own 
fashion or jewelry brands,

00:03:51.022 --> 00:03:52.857
but they also own distributors

00:03:52.857 --> 00:03:55.068
and that's very important because

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.570
Sephora will not be slashing prices

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.865
on Dior products because that
would be too destructive for the brand.

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:04.327
Desirability is something that
really keeps him awake at night.

00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:08.039
He's got to strike the right balance
between selling a lot of these products

00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:11.584
without cheapening their 
perception in the eyes of the consumer.

00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:14.629
In the last ten years, LVMH shares

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:18.007
hit an annual growth rate of about 20%.

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:19.342
But the uninhibited spending

00:04:19.342 --> 00:04:20.510
on luxury goods

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:23.930
stands in stark contrast 
to growing global inequality,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.891
making an escalating class 
struggle especially prominent

00:04:26.891 --> 00:04:28.685
and propelling an 
"Eat the Rich" narrative.

00:04:30.687 --> 00:04:33.064
In April 2023, French protesters

00:04:33.064 --> 00:04:36.109
stormed LVMH’s Paris headquarters

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:40.780
when President Macron tried to
raise the retirement age from 62 to 64.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:45.243
That very same day, shares for the 
company had reached a record high.

00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:48.121
Luxury today is recreating, in a sense,

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:51.124
the idea of elites, the idea of masses,

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:54.669
the idea of a lifestyle that
everybody wants to share.

00:04:54.669 --> 00:04:58.631
Headwinds from a global economic
downturn are also emerging.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:02.927
This sector was priced for perfection.

00:05:02.927 --> 00:05:05.013
Just one little cloud in the sky,

00:05:05.013 --> 00:05:09.517
just one shadow on the board
can actually create this market rout.

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:13.062
Obviously, the consumer is key
to the big luxury brands,

00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:15.940
and the consumer is sort of 
reluctant to spend right now.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:17.775
Potentially trading LVMH,

00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:19.610
it makes some sense because there is

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.487
a reopening consumer driven trade,

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.573
although that's already fizzling,

00:05:23.573 --> 00:05:24.782
it would seem.

00:05:24.782 --> 00:05:29.120
LVMH is particularly exposed to political
and economic swings in Asia

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:34.208
with 37% of its revenue in 
2022 generated from the region.

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:38.713
In May, billions were wiped out
when a slew of economic numbers pointed

00:05:38.713 --> 00:05:40.840
pointed to a fading recovery in China,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.718
largely credited for 
driving the industry's rally.

00:05:43.718 --> 00:05:47.680
The Chinese economy, the 
economic recovery is sputtering.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:48.848
You don't have any concerns

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:51.225
about China's slowdown
at the moment for luxury?

00:05:51.225 --> 00:05:55.480
There were always 
concerns for the world

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:00.526
and finally, our activities are 
growing very well for many years.

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:01.986
So I am optimistic.

00:06:01.986 --> 00:06:05.239
For all his optimism, one 
unanswered question remains:

00:06:05.239 --> 00:06:07.241
Who will lead LVMH 
after Bernard Arnault,

00:06:07.241 --> 00:06:09.660
now in his seventies, retires?

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.331
In this real life ‘Succession’ scenario,
the world is kept guessing

00:06:13.331 --> 00:06:15.917
which of his five children
will take the reins

00:06:15.917 --> 00:06:19.212
as they all assume bigger roles in LVMH.

00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:21.172
Here's who's who in the Arnault clan.

00:06:21.172 --> 00:06:23.132
There's the eldest, Delphine.

00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:27.678
She's the head of LVMH’s second
biggest fashion brand, Christian Dior.

00:06:27.678 --> 00:06:30.765
His second, Antoine, the most 
public-facing executive

00:06:30.765 --> 00:06:35.061
among the siblings, oversees
LVMH’s image and public relations.

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:38.898
From his second marriage, Bernard Arnault had three more children.

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:42.151
Alexandre is the head of products
and communication at Tiffany.

00:06:42.151 --> 00:06:44.821
Frederic heads the watch 
brand Tag Heuer

00:06:44.821 --> 00:06:46.239
and the youngest, Jean,

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:49.075
is in charge of developing Louis Vuitton's watch catalog.

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:51.202
One of the interesting things
about the family

00:06:51.202 --> 00:06:54.288
is that they make a point of 
meeting once a month

00:06:54.288 --> 00:06:56.999
at the LVMH headquarters here in Paris,

00:06:56.999 --> 00:06:58.501
where they have lunches

00:06:58.501 --> 00:07:02.088
and they discuss everything from 
business to personal matters.

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:03.423
It's a nice relationship,

00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:04.424
and working with your family

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:05.716
is very pleasant.

00:07:05.716 --> 00:07:09.720
Now being the son in a 
family such as mine, of course

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.431
is a great responsibility.

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:14.016
Even with this children 
in leadership positions,

00:07:14.016 --> 00:07:16.811
it doesn't look like Arnault 
is ready to retire just yet.

00:07:16.811 --> 00:07:21.315
In 2022, LVMH shareholders voted
to lift the age limit of the CEO,

00:07:21.315 --> 00:07:24.777
i.e. of Bernard Arnault, from 75 to 80.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:28.322
I feel that that's already
built into share price.

00:07:28.322 --> 00:07:32.076
I think that the market overall
expects at some point he will go.

00:07:32.076 --> 00:07:34.495
Versus some of the peers, 
most of the peers,

00:07:34.495 --> 00:07:37.206
LVMH is very well placed for succession.

00:07:37.206 --> 00:07:40.001
Arnault, via his holding company Agache,

00:07:40.001 --> 00:07:43.379
owns 48% of LVMH’s share capital

00:07:43.379 --> 00:07:46.924
but has almost 64% of the voting rights.

00:07:46.924 --> 00:07:48.134
The ownership structure

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:49.719
was tweaked in 2022

00:07:49.719 --> 00:07:51.304
 to ensure the Arnault family

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:53.431
retains its control over the luxury goods

00:07:53.431 --> 00:07:55.016
conglomerate for the long term.

00:07:55.016 --> 00:07:58.436
What I think is really important today
is that once you have your whole family

00:07:58.436 --> 00:08:02.899
set up, trained to the business
and to the family house,

00:08:02.899 --> 00:08:05.359
they stick together against the enemy.

00:08:05.359 --> 00:08:08.571
anyone who's not part of the family
and who would like to have stakes

00:08:08.571 --> 00:08:10.031
in the company.

00:08:10.031 --> 00:08:13.075
The idea is to have a family community

00:08:13.075 --> 00:08:16.412
that is going to stick together
to avoid any type of intrusion.

